# Shell scripts MUST arrive in the working tree with LF endings.
#
# Git stores these files as LF and always has. The damage happens on
# *checkout*: Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` at system scope, so
# without a rule here every Windows clone materialises deploy/*.sh with CRLF.
# `docker build` then COPYs the working-tree file, so the shebang lands in the
# image as `#!/bin/sh` + CR. Linux looks for an interpreter literally named
# "/bin/sh<CR>", fails, and reports the ENOENT against the *script*:
#
#   [FATAL tini (7)] exec /usr/local/bin/trackaroo-entrypoint failed:
#   No such file or directory
#
# which reads like a missing COPY. The container could not start at all.
# Verified: a fresh clone of the parent commit yields CR=162 on
# entrypoint-single.sh; with this file present it yields CR=0.
#
# See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md Part 3 and unit_testing/test_shell_scripts.py.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Consumed by Linux tooling in the image build.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf

# Markdown and Python are edited by tooling that defaults to the platform
# newline on Windows. Pinning them keeps diffs reviewable - a whole-file
# rewrite hides the handful of lines that actually changed.
*.md text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
